  "selection": "Six opt-in risk policies: weights .05/.15/.5 crossed with reserve fractions .15/.3. First prefer nonnegative mean population differences against all development controls in BOTH environments, stable mean energy >= -0.5 versus incumbent, and positive relocation mean energy versus incumbent and matched risk-learning-disabled control. Rank by the smaller of stable/relocation minimum mean energy differences versus frozen, noMemory and neural, also including relocation versus noRisk; tie break by ID. Lock one policy before opening held-out data. If no candidate is eligible, still evaluate the best-ranked exploratory candidate without reselection or promotion.",
  "interpretation": "Human-designed learned reserve-cost auxiliary, not RSI or a learned death probability. Existing observable energy sensor produces max(0,1-energyFraction/reserveThreshold). Energy and reserve heads use aligned discounted TD targets and the SAME greedy next action. Terminal energy future is zero; terminal reserve future uses an explicitly unsafe absorbing state. Two heads pay twice the existing per-record storage/access coefficients; noMemory/global-frozen arms have empty stores, while riskLearning:false and zero-weight controls retain the extra-head charge. Bodies, food and physical energy prices remain unchanged. Reproduction/mutation disabled. No stage increase. Held-out gate requires positive energy lower bound and nonnegative population mean versus required controls in BOTH environments, a positive relocation energy lower bound versus the matched noRisk ablation with nonnegative population difference, stable noRisk noninferiority (-0.5 lower bound) with nonnegative population difference, unchanged separate cue-component retention, stable incumbent noninferiority, and positive relocation advantage over incumbent. The cue task tests the unchanged underlying memory/feature component without reserve labels; it is not evidence of risk-controller whole-body retention."
